  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong ~1030-1035hPa anticyclone centered in southern Ontario\nwill build southward towards the Northeast today in the wake of\na departing shortwave trough. Large scale subsidence will mark \nour sixth straight day with sunny skies and dry conditions.\nNortherly winds in between the departing shortwave trough and\nincoming high will support cool/dry air advection with PWATs\ndropping 1 to 2 standard deviations below normal per the latest\nNAEFS and ENS standardized anomaly. Thus, temperatures today\nwill trend cooler compared to previous days yet remain\nseasonable for late October likely only rising into the mid to\nupper 50s. Although some cirrus clouds increase after Midnight \nas moisture spills overtop a shortwave ridge building out of \nthe Great Lakes, still expecting near ideal radiational cooling \nconditions. There is high confidence in another widespread \nfreeze by early Saturday morning with most areas falling into \nthe mid to upper 20s. Similar to the previous forecast, leaned \non the lower end of guidance for low temperatures to capture the\ncooling potential. Canadian high pressure builds overhead for\nSaturday and Sunday leading to a stellar weekend. Seasonable \ntemperatures on Saturday trend slightly above normal on Sunday \nas zonal flow ensues supporting west to southwesterly return \nflow. Mild temperatures continue into Monday ahead of a moisture\nstarved cold front. High confidence in our dry stretch \ncontinuing through the middle of next week with the latest \nprobabilistic guidance showing 0% chance for any measurable (at\nleast 0.01\") rain through Wednesday with less than a 10% chance\nfocused mainly north and west of the Capital District for at \nleast a 0.10\" of rain in the 24 hr period 8am Wed to 8am Thurs.",
